          "content": "Based on your current infrastructure and progress, here's what you should build next:\n\n## Recommended: Application Load Balancer (ALB) 🎯\n\n**Why this makes sense:**\n- Your EC2 instances are in **private subnets** (no direct internet access)\n- You have **multiple instances** but no way to distribute traffic\n- ALB will expose your web servers properly\n- Completes Phase 2 before moving to databases\n\n## What You'll Build:\n\n```\nInternet\n    ↓\nApplication Load Balancer (public subnets)\n    ↓\nTarget Group\n    ↓\nEC2 Instances (private subnets)\n```\n\n## Benefits:\n- ✅ Single entry point for your application\n- ✅ Health checks for instances\n- ✅ SSL/TLS termination (future)\n- ✅ Traffic distribution across AZs\n- ✅ Instances stay secure in private subnets\n\n## What You'll Create:\n\n1. **ALB Module** (`module/loadbalancer/`)\n   - Application Load Balancer\n   - Target Group\n   - Listener (HTTP:80)\n   - Security Group for ALB\n\n2. **Updates Needed:**\n   - Add ALB security group (allow 80/443 from internet)\n   - Update EC2 security group (allow traffic from ALB only)\n   - Add outputs for ALB DNS name\n\n## Estimated Time: 30-45 minutes\n\n## Alternative Options:\n\n**Option B: Database Layer** (if you need data persistence)\n- RDS PostgreSQL\n- ElastiCache Redis\n- S3 buckets\n\n**Option C: Monitoring** (if you want visibility first)\n- CloudWatch dashboards\n- Alarms\n- SNS notifications\n\n**My recommendation: Start with ALB** - it's the missing piece to make your current infrastructure accessible and production-ready.\n\nWant me to help you build the ALB module?"
